### Summary

[read_meshes_reproduce.tar.gz](https://github.com/user-attachments/files/20605340/read_meshes_reproduce.tar.gz)
[read_meshes_reports.tar.gz](https://github.com/user-attachments/files/20605341/read_meshes_reports.tar.gz)

The program might experience a heap-buffer-overflow in the read_meshes function in `HL1MDLLoader.cpp`. The issue appears to occur when handling meshes, vertices, normals, and bones, especially when accessing out-of-bounds memory during operations involving these components. Below are the lines that might be contributing to this problem:
```C++
// assimp/code/AssetLib/MDL/HalfLife/HL1MDLLoader.cpp:611
scene_->mNumMeshes += pmodel->nummesh;
// assimp/code/AssetLib/MDL/HalfLife/HL1MDLLoader.cpp:769
bind_pose_vertices[k] = temp_bones_[pvertbone[k]].absolute_transform * aiVector3D(vert[0], vert[1], vert[2]);
// assimp/code/AssetLib/MDL/HalfLife/HL1MDLLoader.cpp:775
const aiMatrix4x4 normal_matrix = aiMatrix4x4(temp_bones_[pnormbone[k]].absolute_transform).Inverse().Transpose();
// assimp/code/AssetLib/MDL/HalfLife/HL1MDLLoader.cpp:791
float texcoords_s_scale = 1.0f / (float)ptexture[pskinref[pmesh->skinref]].width;
// assimp/code/AssetLib/MDL/HalfLife/HL1MDLLoader.cpp:816
const int bone = pvertbone[input_trivert->vertindex];
// assimp/code/AssetLib/MDL/HalfLife/HL1MDLLoader.cpp:934
scene_bone->mName = temp_bones_[bone_index].node->mName;
```
These lines might be accessing memory that has already been freed, leading to undefined behavior such as heap-buffer-overflow. Detailed analysis of the specific memory access patterns within these lines is necessary to identify the root cause.

### Platform
 - OS: `Ubuntu 22.04.5 LTS`
 - Clang version: `Ubuntu clang version 16.0.6 (++20231112100510+7cbf1a259152-1~exp1~20231112100554.106)`
 - Version commit `b3a47a68fa0a379b1fa0788e4a5f448111cd2012`

### To Reproduce

```shell
git clone https://github.com/assimp/assimp.git && cd assimp && git checkout b3a47a68fa0a379b1fa0788e4a5f448111cd2012

export CC=clang CXX=clang++ CFLAGS='-fsanitize=address,fuzzer -g -O1' CXXFLAGS='-fsanitize=address,fuzzer -g -O1' LDFLAGS="-fuse-ld=lld"

cmake CMakeLists.txt -G -DBUILD_SHARED_LIBS=OFF -DASSIMP_BUILD_ZLIB=ON  -DASSIMP_BUILD_TESTS=OFF -DASSIMP_BUILD_ASSIMP_TOOLS=OFF -DASSIMP_BUILD_SAMPLES=OFF

$CXX $CXXFLAGS -fsanitize=fuzzer -I./include fuzz/assimp_fuzzer.cc -o ./assimp_fuzzer  ./lib/libassimp.a ./contrib/zlib/libzlibstatic.a

./assimp_fuzzer /PATH/TO/poc
```

### ASAN report
in [read_meshes_reports.tar.gz](https://github.com/user-attachments/files/20605341/read_meshes_reports.tar.gz)

**Describe the bug**
Integer overflow can happen in function `Q3DImporter::InternReadFile()` if two high numbers are provided in the multiplication in line [375](https://github.com/assimp/assimp/blob/8621d5243070eb85ad7411634b5463731fdac7ed/code/AssetLib/Q3D/Q3DLoader.cpp#L375).

The resulting value is then used to set the pointer of a stream in line [382](https://github.com/assimp/assimp/blob/8621d5243070eb85ad7411634b5463731fdac7ed/code/AssetLib/Q3D/Q3DLoader.cpp#L382), which will then not behave correctly as a result.

Also in this case, this overflow can be avoided by checking the operands for overflow before triggering it, and raising an error if any is found, e.g., by adding:
```
    unsigned int tmp1 = stream.GetI4();
    unsigned int tmp2 = stream.GetI4();
    if(tmp1 != 0 && tmp2 > UINT_MAX/tmp1) {
        throw DeadlyImportError("Quick3D: [...]");
    }
     unsigned int temp = (unsigned int)(tmp1 * tmp2);
```
Instead of performing the multiplication.

**Reproduce**
You can reproduce the error by using the file in the [attached archive](https://github.com/user-attachments/files/20613533/2.zip). We tested your fuzzing harness compiling with LLVM 20 and AFL++ 4.32, on Ubuntu 20.04. To detect this issue, UBSan is needed.

**Describe the bug**
I want to import georeferenced models that have 13 digits coordinates (like `7502223.336235`). I've compiled assimp v6.0.4 with `-DASSIMP_DOUBLE_PRECISION=ON` flag via vcpkg custom triplet. I am using `double` and `aiVector3d` where applicable.

**To Reproduce**
1. Compile assimp with `-DASSIMP_DOUBLE_PRECISION=ON` flag
```cpp
	Assimp::Importer importer;
	const aiScene* scene = importer.ReadFile(path, aiProcess_Triangulate);

	unsigned long long totalVerts = 0;
	for (unsigned int i = 0; i < scene->mNumMeshes; i++) {
		totalVerts += scene->mMeshes[i]->mNumVertices;
	}

	glm::dvec3 bbMin(DBL_MAX);
	glm::dvec3 bbMax(-DBL_MAX);

	for (unsigned int m = 0; m < scene->mNumMeshes; m++) {
		aiMesh* mesh = scene->mMeshes[m];

		for (unsigned int i = 0; i < mesh->mNumVertices; i++) {
			const aiVector3d& v = mesh->mVertices[i];

			bbMin.x = std::min(bbMin.x, v.x);
			bbMin.y = std::min(bbMin.y, v.y);
			bbMin.z = std::min(bbMin.z, v.z);

			bbMax.x = std::max(bbMax.x, v.x);
			bbMax.y = std::max(bbMax.y, v.y);
			bbMax.z = std::max(bbMax.z, v.z);
		}
	}
	processNode(scene->mRootNode, scene);
}

void Model::processNode(aiNode* node, const aiScene* scene) {
	for (unsigned int i = 0; i < node->mNumMeshes; i++) {
		aiMesh* mesh = scene->mMeshes[node->mMeshes[i]];
		meshes.push_back(processMesh(mesh, scene));
	}
    // ....
}

Mesh Model::processMesh(aiMesh* mesh, const aiScene* scene) {
	std::vector<glm::vec4> positions(mesh->mNumVertices);
	std::vector<unsigned int> indices;
	indices.reserve(mesh->mNumFaces * 3);

	for (unsigned int i = 0; i < mesh->mNumVertices; i++) {
		const aiVector3d& v = mesh->mVertices[i];
		glm::dvec3 posD(v.x, v.y, v.z);
		glm::dvec3 local = posD - modelOrigin;
		positions[i] = glm::vec4(local, 1.0f);
	}

	for (unsigned int i = 0; i < mesh->mNumFaces; i++) {
		const aiFace& face = mesh->mFaces[i];
		indices.push_back(face.mIndices[0]); // Triangulation is enabled
		indices.push_back(face.mIndices[1]);
		indices.push_back(face.mIndices[2]);
	}
	// DO SOMETHING WITH GEOMETRY
}
```

The code imports the model with double precision, centres it and returns a float position. Returned positions are in the range between -100 and 100, so the float here is not the problem - the problem is somewhere before - in assimp code. 

